---
const rows = [
  ["We stayed indoors.", "because the wind was fierce", "Cause"],
  ["We set off.", "when the rain stopped", "Time"],
  ["We will play.", "if the pitch is dry", "Condition"],
  ["She finished the race.", "although she was tired", "Contrast"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Clauses - Main and subordinate</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>Main clause</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>main clause</strong> makes sense on its own: <strong>The lights went out.</strong> Two main
      clauses may be joined: “The lights went out, but the torches still worked.”
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>FSCE check.</strong> If you can split at the conjunction and both parts stand alone, you have
      main clause + coordinating conjunction + main clause.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Subordinate clause</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>subordinate clause</strong> adds information and cannot normally stand alone. It may tell
      when, why, or under what condition something happened.
    </p>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Main clause</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Subordinate clause</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([main, sub, meaning]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{main}</td>
              <td>{sub}</td>
              <td>{meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    <strong>Because, when, if, although, while</strong> and <strong>before</strong> can introduce these
    clauses. The clause can move: “When the rain stopped, we set off” / “We set off when the rain stopped.”
    A comma usually follows an introductory subordinate clause.
  </p>
  <p class="try-it">
    <strong>Try it.</strong> Would changing <em>because</em> to <em>although</em> change the story in “We
    moved inside because it rained”? Explain the relationship, not just the word.
  </p>
</div>
